Creating Entrance and Exit Controls
Create Entrance and Exit Controls
You can modify the built-in transfer behavior of the material flow objects by programming an Entrance
Control and/or an Exit Control.
The material flow object calls the Method, whose name you entered, as soon as a MU intends to enter or
exit the respective object. Be aware that these controls override the standard transfer behavior, meaning
that you yourself have to make sure that the MU moves to the correct station!
You can use control methods in a number of ways. You might, for example, count the number of
incoming MUs and then, after a specified number has been reached, move the following MUs to a different
successor.
You will program an Entrance Control and/or an Exit Control in a Method. This can either be:
• An object of type Method 
 , which can be located in a folder in the Class Library or in a Frame.
If you want a number of objects, which are located in the same Frame, to call the same Method, you
typically use a Method object, which you insert into that Frame.
If you want a number of objects, which are located in different Frames, to call the same Method, you
typically use a Method object, which you place in the Class Library.
• A user-defined attribute of data type method, which only the object to which you added it, can use.
When you want all instances of a class to provide the same control, you typically create that control as a
user-defined attribute in the class.
Creating Entrance and Exit Controls
10-328 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To tell Plant Simulation which Method to use, do one of the following. The process is the same for
Entrance and Exit and Backward Entrance and Backward Exit Controls:
• Click the button 
  and select a Method in a Frame in the dialog Select Object. By default Plant
Simulation enters the relative path into the text box.
To activate the entrance control of the point-oriented objects before the standard actions are started,
when a part enters the object, select Before Actions. Standard actions are, for example, starting the
Processing Time or the Set-up Time, requesting Services through the importer, or changing the
assembly table of the AssemblyStation. You can then, for example, change the Processing Time in the
Entrance Control, which affects processing of the entering part.
• Select the Method in a Frame, hold down Shift and Ctrl, and drag it to the text box and drop it there.
Here Plant Simulation enters the absolute path into the text box.
Create Entrance and Exit Controls
Plant Simulation Help 10-329
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To open the dialog of the object, whose name you entered into the text box, either press F2 or
right-click the text box and select Open Object.
• Right-click into the text box and select Create Control on the context menu to create a control method
as a user-defined attribute of this object.
◦ Type a meaningful name into the text box and select Create Control. Plant Simulation then inserts
self.Name_you_typed_in_for_the_control, such as self.ExitControlEnginePlant. Or
◦ Select Create Control. Plant Simulation then inserts self.OnBuilt_in_name_of_the_control,
such as self.OnEntrance.
Type the source code of this control into the Method that opens.
To open the Method editor, either press F2 or click the right mouse button into the text box and select
Open Object and select the object or press Shift and double-click in the text box.
Be aware that a control you create with Create Control is a user-defined attribute of the respective
object, not a Method object, which you can open in the Frame.
To open the control from the tab User-defined double-click it and then click Open.
◦ To delete this control, delete the user-defined attribute. Deleting the name from the text box just
deletes the name, but keeps the user-defined attribute.
Create Entrance and Exit Controls
10-330 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
The Exit Control can have an optional parameter of data type object. When the Method has this
parameter, then the successor object, which pulls the MU because the MU is contained in the blocking
list of the object, will be assigned to this parameter. The MU will be pulled, for example, when the
successor becomes available again or when its entrance is opened.
You can:
• Define Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
• Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
