Defining Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
Define Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
You can define controls for point-oriented objects,
Point-oriented objects are the material flow objects that provide one or more processing stations, but do
not have a length and do not take the length of the MU into account.
The Station 
 , the ParallelStation 
 , the AssemblyStation 
 , the DismantleStation 
 , the Buffer 
 , the
PlaceBuffer 
 , the Store 
 , the Sorter 
 , the Source 
 , and the Drain 
  are point-oriented.
Defining Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
Plant Simulation Help 10-331
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Plant Simulation activates the Entrance Control of the point-oriented objects, such as the Station 
 ,
once the MU has entered the object in its entirety. This is the default setting for which the check box
 is cleared.
To activate the entrance control before the standard actions are started, when a part enters the object,
select Before Actions. Standard actions are, for example, starting the Processing Time or the Set-up
Time, requesting Services through the importer, or changing the Assembly Table of the AssemblyStation.
You can then, for example, change the Processing Time in the Entrance Control, which affects processing
of the entering part.
Plant Simulation activates the Exit Control, when a MU exits the object.
The check boxes Front and Rear set, when the MU triggers the Method.
• Select Front to activate the control as soon as the MU is ready to exit the object. The Exit Control then
has to move the part to another object, as the built-in behavior of the material flow object, i.e., moving
the MU on to the successors along the Connectors, has been overridden by the Exit Control.
Note
The same MU can call the front-triggered exit control more than once, if that MU could not
exit the object and entered itself into the Blocking List of the destination object. As soon as the
Define Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
10-332 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

destination object is ready to receive the MU, the MU is assigned a new Out event, which triggers the
front-triggered exit control again.
Select Rear to activate the control as soon as the rear of the MU has completely exited the object. This
control does not override the built-in behavior of how MUs move to the successor of the current object.
Note
Plant Simulation calls the rear-activated exit control once only. It does not override the default exit
strategy.
Note
Compare the Out, OutEnd and Reference events in the List of scheduled events.
• The check boxes Front and Rear are not mutually exclusive. If you Front and Rear, Plant Simulation
executes the respective control in both cases.
You can, for example:
• Change the Processing Time in the Entrance Control
• Change the Importer Services in the Entrance Control
• Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
• Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
Compare Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
