which sets when the two containers are delivered.
Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-343
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ The SourceParts produces the parts which are loaded onto the pallet.
Enter the Interval. We entered an interval of 20 seconds, i.e., 0:20.
Select the MU Selection. We selected Random and then the name of the table, which we inserted
before, namely the DataTable named MyParts. Selecting the table automatically applied the correct
format. We typed in the data shown in the screenshot below, which sets the frequencies at which the
two parts are created.
Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
10-344 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ Insert and configure the station which processes the parts. We used a ParallelStation and increased its
capacity to 100 parts, i.e., we entered an X-Dimension and a Y-Dimension of 10 each. We did not
change any of the other settings.
◦ Insert and configure the AssemblyStation which assembles the parts that arrive from the Sources.
We assemble the parts according to the Assembly Table. As we want to attach the parts, we selected
the Assembly Mode > Attach MUs.
Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-345
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ Select and program the Entrance Control. We selected the method MyEntranceControl, which we
inserted before, as the Entrance Control and selected Before Actions.
We typed in the source code below. It creates the local variable assyList. It then checks the names of
the containers, A or B, and writes the name and the amount for that part, MyPartX or MyPartY, into
the assyList. It finally sets the local variable assyList as the assembly list of the AssemblyStation.
The source code looks like this:
var assyList: table[string,integer]
assyList.create
switch @.Name
   case "A" 
       assyList.writeRow(1,1, "MyPartX",1)
       assyList.writeRow(1,2, "MyPartY",2)
   case "B" 
       assyList.writeRow(1,1, "MyPartX",2)
Change the Assembly Table in the Entrance Control
10-346 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

       assyList.writeRow(1,2, "MyPartY",1)
end
Assembly.AssemblyTable := assyList
◦ Insert the Conveyor, which transports the assembled parts, and the Drain, which removes them from
the plant.
◦ Run the simulation and watch what happens. To follow how the events are processed during the
simulation, you can click 
  in the EventController and then click 
  in the EventDebugger.
Compare the event list with the parts, which the Entrance Control entered into the assembly table.
Click 
  to open the Assembly Table.
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Back to Define Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
You can determine how a station moves the parts, which it has processed, on to its successors in the Exit
Control.
Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-347
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

In our example the Source produces 1000 parts and moves these on to the Station. We are going to count
the number of incoming parts in the Exit Control, and then, after a certain number has been reached,
move the following parts on to a different successor.
The finished simulation model looks like this:
To create the model, proceed like this:
• Insert and configure the Source which produces the parts. We selected and entered the settings below
and did not change any other settings.
Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
10-348 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Insert and configure the stations which process the parts. We inserted four Stations. We use them with
their default settings, except for the Station that distributes the parts across its successors.
• Select and program the Exit Control for this station.
• We selected the Method named MyExitControl and entered the source code below.
var n: integer
n := Station.StatNumIn
if n <= 100 
Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
Plant Simulation Help 10-349
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

   @.move(Station1)
elseif n <= 300 
   @.move(Station2)
else
   @.move(Station3)
end
We declared the Variable named n and the data type integer. We typed in n :=
Station.StatNumIn. This reads the statistics value StatNumIn of the Station. We then pressed Enter
again, changed to the Template menu and selected if... elseif... end. This inserted an if... elseif... end-
statement into the source code and saved us from typing it in. It also showed us, with the expressions
highlighted in red, which parts of the source code we have to replace with our own instructions.
◦ First, we replaced the condition with our condition, namely n less than or equal to 100, i.e., we typed
in n <= 100.
◦ Next, we replaced the then_branch with @.move(Station1). This instruction tells Plant Simulation
to move the first 100 parts to Station1.
◦ Then, we replaced the elseif condition with our condition, n less than or equal to 300, i.e., we typed
in n = <300. We replaced the remaining then_branch with @.move(Station2). This moves the
next 200 parts to the station Station2.
◦ We then pressed Enter, typed in else and pressed Enter again.
◦ Finally, we typed in @.move(Station3). This moves the remaining 700 parts to Station3. The @
sign in our source code identifies the part, which is going to be moved.
Run the simulation and check the statistics report. It shows that the parts are distributed as we
programmed them to do: The first 100 parts moved to Station1, the next 200 to Station2, and the
remaining 700 to Station3.
Distribute Parts with an Exit Control
10-350 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Compare Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
Back to Create Entrance and Exit Controls
Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
You can define controls for length-oriented objects.
The length-oriented objects Track 
 , TwoLaneTrack 
 , Conveyor 
 , Turnplate 
 , Turntable 
 ,
AngularConverter 
 , and Converter 
  take their own length and the length of the MUs, which move on
them, into account during the simulation runs.
In addition to the forward Entrance Control and the forward Exit Control, they provide a Backward
Entrance Control and a Backward Exit Control. Plant Simulation activates these controls, when the
Transporter 
  drives in reverse on them.
Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
Plant Simulation Help 10-351
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
The Transporter backs up on the Track, it does not turn around. This means that it drives in reverse with
its front end still pointing in the direction of the flow of materials instead of its front end pointing in the
opposite direction!
Note
The Front of the part always moves toward the end of the length-oriented object into the direction
with which you inserted it along the direction of motion of the material flow. When you insert a
length-oriented object, for example a Conveyor, from left to right, the Front of the part is located on the
right-hand side and moves toward the right. When you insert a length-oriented object from right to left,
the Front of the part is located on the left-hand side and moves toward the left.
Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
10-352 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

This also applies to parts which move backward when you select the check box Backward of the
Conveyor or the Transporter! The Front of the part keeps on pointing toward the end of the length-
oriented object into the direction with which you inserted it along the direction of motion of the
material flow.
Plant Simulation activates the Entrance Control you entered:
• When the Front or the Rear of the MU has entered the length-oriented object.
Remember that a MU, for which you entered a length, does not move onto these objects all at once, but
continually. Depending on the length and the speed of the MU, there will a delay between the time the
front and the time the rear of the MU enters the object.
◦ Select Front to activate the control as soon as the front of the MU has entered, i.e., is located on the
object. This means that changing the processing time in the entrance control does not affect the MU
that already entered the object. To set the processing time depending on the MU that arrives, define
the processing time in a formula.
◦ Select Rear to activate the control as soon as the rear of the MU has entered the object.
Plant Simulation activates the Backward Entrance Controls:
When the Front or the Rear of the Transporter has moved onto the length-oriented objects Track,
TwoLaneTrack, or Conveyor at their entrance while it backs up.
Remember that a Transporter, for which you entered a length, does not move onto these objects all at
once while backing up, but continually. Depending on the length and the speed of the Transporter, there
will a delay between the time the back and the time its front is located on the object:
• Select Rear to activate the control as soon as the rear of the Transporter has moved onto the object
while driving in reverse.
• Select Front to activate the control as soon as the front of the Transporter has moved onto the object
driving moving in reverse.
• You can also select both check boxes, Front and Rear, for the entrance control and the exit control.
Plant Simulation activates the control as soon as the Front or the Rear of the Transporter enter or exit
the object.
Plant Simulation activates the Backward Exit Controls:
When the Front or the Rear of the Transporter has moved onto the length-oriented objects at their exit
while it drives in reverse.
The check boxes Front and Rear set, when the Transporter triggers the Methods.
• Select Rear to activate the control as soon as the back of the Transporter has moved onto the object at
its exit while driving in reverse.
Define Controls for Length-Oriented Objects
Plant Simulation Help 10-353
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
Plant Simulation calls the rear-activated exit control once only. It does not override the default exit
strategy.
• Select Front to activate the control as soon as the front of the Transporter has moved onto the object
at its exit while driving in reverse. The exit control then has to move the part to another object, as the
built-in behavior of the e, i.e., moving the MU on to the successors along the Connectors, has been
overridden by the exit control.
Note
The same MU can call the front-triggered Exit Control more than once, if that MU could not
exit the object and entered itself into the Blocking List of the destination object. As soon as the
destination object is ready to receive the MU, the MU is assigned a new Out event, which triggers the
front-triggered Exit Control again.
• You can also select both check boxes, Front and Rear, for the entrance control and the exit control.
Plant Simulation activates the control as soon as the Front or the Rear of the MU enter or exit the
object.
Compare Define Controls for Point-Oriented Objects
Compare Model Transport Systems
Back to Create Entrance and Exit Controls
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
The material flow objects have built-in sensors, i.e., the entrance controls and the exit controls that the
arriving or the leaving MUs trigger. A triggered sensor calls the Method object, whose name you specified
as the Entrance Control or as the Exit Control. These user-defined controls will then replace the built-in
properties of the object.
For the length-oriented objects Track 
 , TwoLaneTrack 
 , Turntable 
 , and Conveyor 
 , for the
Transporter 
 , and for the Tank 
  you yourself can, in addition to the entrance controls and to the
exit controls, define sensor controls anywhere on the object. The Track/TwoLaneTrack and the Conveyor
activate the Methods you assigned to the sensor, once a MU passes the sensor, which in your plant might
be a light barrier, or a similar device. In the Method assigned to the control you can program the action you
want Plant Simulation to take. You might, for example define the conditions that apply when the parts are
transferred onto the next material flow object, or you might change the target velocity, and the icon, of a
Transporter, when its front reaches the sensor.
Note
The Front of the part always moves toward the end of the length-oriented object into the direction
with which you inserted it along the direction of motion of the material flow. When you insert a
length-oriented object, for example a Conveyor, from left to right, the Front of the part is located on the
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
10-354 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

right-hand side and moves toward the right. When you insert a length-oriented object from right to left,
the Front of the part is located on the left-hand side and moves toward the left.
This also applies to parts which move backward when you select the check box Backward of the
Conveyor or the Transporter! The Front of the part keeps on pointing toward the end of the length-
oriented object into the direction with which you inserted it along the direction of motion of the
material flow.
To create sensors in the dialog of the length-oriented objects:
• Click the position at which you would like to insert the sensor with the right mouse button and select
Create Sensor on the context menu. Specify the data defining to the sensor into the dialog that opens.
• Or click the tab Controls.
Note
The Transporter shows the button Sensors on the tab Load Bay, when you select Track or Line. Click
Apply to activate the button.
• Click 
  and select the name of the control method in which you programmed the action you would like
the Transporter to take. In our example, we want it to turn around, when its front passes the sensor.
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
Plant Simulation Help 10-355
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To insert additional sensors, click Sensors.
• To create a new sensor and open the dialog Sensor, click New 
 .
◦ The ID in the title bar of the dialog is the number that Plant Simulation automatically assigns to
the sensor, when it creates it. You can use this unique identifier to access that sensor from within
Methods.
◦ Select the type of position, Relative or Length, from the drop-down list and specify the position of
the sensor.
◊ For Relative you can specify a value between 0 and 1, and Plant Simulation show 0..1 to the right
of the text box.
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
10-356 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◊ For Length you can specify a value between 0 and the length of the object.
Plant Simulation uses the length unit you selected under File > Model Settings/Preferences > Units
> Length. When you specify an invalid value, Plant Simulation changes the color of the text box to
red.
◦ When you define a sensor, you can select when a MU triggers the sensor:
Always, independent of the destination of the MU.
Or Only when the MU has the same destination as the one you entered into the sensor.
Automatic Routing of the Transporter and Automatic Routing of Part and Container also includes
this destination object. When you assign a Destination to the MU while automatic routing is active, it
drives to the sensor. Once there it activates the Sensor Control. For the Transporter it also activates
the Destination Control. The same destination object can be defined in several sensors. It may also
be directly reached via different routes. Even when a direct route leads to the destination object,
the MU drives to the nearest sensor, when this sensor can be reached on a shorter route than the
destination object itself.
◦ Specify the name of the object to which the Transporter drives into the text box Destination.
◦ Click 
  and select the name of the Method 
 , which the sensor calls, in the dialog Select Object. To
open the dialog of the object, whose name you entered into the text box, click in the text box, and
press F2.
The source code of our Method named accelerate reads like this:
@.Speed := 50
@.currIcon := “car_fast”
As soon as the sensor calls this Method, it passes the Sensor ID as parameter. When the Method
expects an integer parameter, the sensor passes the Sensor ID to the Method. If you do not specify an
integer parameter, the Method will be called without a parameter.
Or you can right-click in the text box. Then, select Create Control on the context menu to create a
control method, which is a user-defined attribute of data type method of this object:
◊ Type a meaningful name into the text box and select Create Control. Plant Simulation then inserts
self.Name_you_typed_in_for_the_control, such as self.MySensorControl.
◊ Select Create Control. Plant Simulation then inserts
self.OnBuilt_in_name_of_the_control, such as self.OnSelect.
◊ To delete this control, delete the user-defined attribute; deleting the name from the text box just
deletes the name, but keeps the user-defined attribute.
Type the source code of this control into the Method that opens.
◦ Select Front, so that the front of the MU activates the Method. You can also select Front and Rear.
Then both the front and the rear of the MU activate the Method.
◦ Select Rear, so that the rear of the MU activates the Method. You can also select Front and Rear.
Then both the front and the rear of the MU activate the Method.
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
Plant Simulation Help 10-357
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ Select Booking Point, so that the Booking Point Length of the MU calls the Method.
◦ Click OK to apply your settings and to close the dialog.
• When you click the Close 
  button, Plant Simulation updates and shows the number of sensors on
the button 
  in the dialog.
• To edit the sensor, which you selected in the list, click Edit. Or double-click the row that defines the
sensor in the list.
• To delete the sensor, which you selected in the list, click Delete.
• To open the sensor that Plant Simulation inserts as a red line into the icon of the object, double-click
the red line and change the settings in the dialog that opens. Or click the sensor with the right mouse
button and select Open Sensor in the context menu.
Note
You might have to zoom in to see the red line symbolizing the sensor position.
• To show a tooltip with information about the sensor, drag the mouse over the red line designating it on
the object.
Create a Sensor [material flow objects]
