Create and Delete an Observer
When you create your simulation model, it will often become necessary to trigger certain actions, when
the watchable values of attributes, read-only attributes, or methods of objects change.
To accomplish this, you can create observers for most of the built-in objects.
Create and Delete an Observer
Plant Simulation Help 10-359
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

This observer watches the value of an attribute/read-only attribute/method and then executes one or
several methods when the value changes. This method can either be a Method object, which you insert
into a Frame, or a user-defined attribute of data type method.
Note
This only works, if the attribute/read-only attribute/method of the object is watchable. The column
Watchable in the window Show Attributes and Methods shows this.
Note
As a rule observer methods are called after all other controls. If you want to react to state changes
before other controls, you have to use the stopuntil-instruction or the waituntil-instruction instead of
an observer.
You can use observers for a wide range of applications, for example:
• Watch the read-only attribute named NumMUs when a certain number of MUs is located on the object.
• Watch the read-only attribute named Empty to check if an object is empty or not.
In our example, we show how to move the contents of Station1 to Station2, when the value of the
read-only attribute named Occupied of Station1 changes.
Create and Delete an Observer
10-360 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Open the dialog of Station1. Select Tools > Edit Observers.
• To create the observer, click New in the dialog Observers.
• To select the attribute/read-only attribute/method whose value you want to watch, click 
  next to
Attribute in the dialog Select Observed Value and Method to be Executed. Then, select the attribute
in the dialog Select Object. We selected Occupied.
Create and Delete an Observer
Plant Simulation Help 10-361
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To select the Method that will be executed, when the value of the attribute/read-only attribute/method
changes, click 
  next to Method. Then, select the Method in the dialog Select Object. We selected the
Method named occupiedObserver in which we programmed our transfer behavior.
Create and Delete an Observer
10-362 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

As the Method you can also use a user-defined attribute of data type method. To do so, right-click
into the text box Method and select the command Create Control. In this case Plant Simulation
automatically formats the Method.
• The dialog Observers then shows that our observer consists of the watched read-only attribute
Occupied and of the method occupiedObserver. The asterisk (*) shows that we created the observer
in Station1, meaning that it is not inherited from its origin.
• The source code of our method occupiedObserver looks like this:
param attribute: string, oldValue: any
if ?.occupied
   ?.cont.move(Station2)
end
◦ First, we declare the two parameters, which are passed to the Method:
◊ The name of the attribute/read-only attribute/method whose watchable value changed. This allows
you to use a single Method as the method to be called for several attributes.
◊ The previous value of the attribute/read-only attribute/method. This way you can still access the
previous value after the executed method changed it to the new value.
◦ Then, we tell Plant Simulation to check if Station1 is occupied, i.e., if it contains a part. If this is the
case, we want it to move the part to Station2. Within the called Method, we can use the anonymous
identifiers ? and @ to address the object, whose read-only-attribute changed, Station1 in our case.
• To delete the observer, you can either click Remove in the dialog or program a method.
For our example above we entered the following source code:
Station1.removeObserver("Occupied","OccupiedObserver")
 // "Occupied" is the name of the observer, "OccupiedObserver" is the 
name of the method
• To open the dialog for selecting the watched value, you can also double-click the text box Observed
Value in the dialog Observers.
Create and Delete an Observer
Plant Simulation Help 10-363
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To open the window of the Method, which will be executed, you can also double-click the text box
Method.
